A const handle parameter (T@ const &in) is treated as incompatible with T@ &in in funcdef signature matching, so array::Sort and every funcdef conversion reject shipped code the engine compiles


## Reported by Max, 2026-09-01, against a shipped plugin that compiles in the game

    ~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-ghosts-plus-plus
    src/Interface.as:641:31: error: No matching signatures to 'array::Sort(SortGhosts)'

The declaration at `src/Interface.as:616`:

    bool SortGhosts(const Json::Value@ const &in a, const Json::Value@ const &in b)

called on a `Json::Value@[]`. **This is the only error the checker reports for
that plugin** (plus 2 signed/unsigned warnings and 118 hidden info).

## Minimal repro — the discriminator is the TRAILING `const`

    bool CmpA(const Json::Value@ const &in a, const Json::Value@ const &in b) { return true; }
    bool CmpB(const Json::Value@ &in a,       const Json::Value@ &in b)       { return true; }
    bool CmpC(Json::Value@ const &in a,       Json::Value@ const &in b)       { return true; }

    Json::Value@[] arr;
    arr.Sort(CmpA);   // ERROR
    arr.Sort(CmpB);   // ok
    arr.Sort(CmpC);   // ERROR

`CmpB` — the only one WITHOUT a trailing `const` — is the only one accepted.
Note the two `const`s mean different things: the leading one is *handle to const
object*, the trailing one is *const handle*. Only the trailing one triggers this.

## It is FAR more general than `array::Sort`

Every funcdef path rejects it, so this is not a template-member-funcdef bug:

    argument 1 of `Less`: expected `bool (const Json::Value@ &in, const Json::Value@ &in)`,
                          got `bool (const Json::Value@ const &in, const Json::Value@ const &in)`
    argument 1 of `LessC`: expected `bool (const C@ &in, const C@ &in)`,
                           got `bool (const C@ const &in, const C@ const &in)`
    Can't implicitly convert from 'bool(const Json::Value@ const &in, ...)' to 'Less@'
    No matching signatures to 'array::Sort(CmpC)'

Confirmed for an engine type (`Json::Value@`) and a workspace class (`C@`), via
explicit funcdef construction, implicit handle assignment, and the template
member. **The parser handles the syntax fine** — the rendered signature above
shows `const Json::Value@ const &in` round-tripping correctly. The defect is in
signature COMPATIBILITY, not parsing.

## The engine is the authority and it has NOT been probed yet

Max reports this as a false positive and the plugin ships and compiles, which is
strong evidence — but it is not a measurement, and this batch has spent a night
proving the difference. **Probe before fixing.** The game is up, RemoteBuild
works (B097), and `scripts/probe_const_receiver.py` /
`probe_engine_const_receiver.py` are the models.

The question to put to the engine, per construct:

- Does a function with `T@ const &in` params bind to a funcdef declaring
  `T@ &in`? (the reported case — expect ACCEPT)
- Does it bind the other way — `T@ &in` function to a `T@ const &in` funcdef?
  **Ask this too.** If the engine is asymmetric, a symmetric fix is wrong.
- Is the leading `const` (`const T@` vs `T@`) still exact, as B110 established?
  **This is the control that stops the fix widening into B110's territory** — do
  not relax handle-to-const alongside const-handle without measuring both.
- The same three for `&out`, `&inout` and by-value, and for a return type.

## Likely location, unverified

B110 made `funcdef_types_compatible` exact by dropping an `is_convertible`
fallback on the primitive arm, and B102 synthesises the template member
funcdef's signature per call site. The rendered strings above show both sides
computed correctly, so the suspect is the comparison — specifically whether
`ParamMode`/`TypeRepr` carries the const-handle bit and whether the comparison
should ignore it.

**Do not fix by loosening the comparison generally.** B110 exists because it was
too loose, and the B110 tests are the regression guard: if a fix makes any B110
case pass that should fail, the fix is wrong.

## Test axes — generalise, per repo CLAUDE.md

Cross every combination rather than fixing the reported spelling:

- Param modes: `&in`, `&out`, `&inout`, by value.
- Const positions, all four: `T@`, `const T@`, `T@ const`, `const T@ const`.
- Element/param types: engine handle (`Json::Value@`), workspace class handle
  (`C@`), interface handle, primitive (`int`), value class, enum, `array<T>@`,
  `dictionary@`.
- Call paths: explicit funcdef construction `Less(f)`, implicit assignment
  `Less@ h = f;`, passing as an argument, and the template member
  (`array<T>::Sort`, 2-arg and 3-arg forms, `SortAsc`/`SortDesc`).
- Return types as well as parameters.
- **Negative controls that must still FAIL:** wrong arity, wrong return type,
  wrong element type, and B110's exactness cases. A fix that makes everything
  match passes a suite that only checks things start working.

## Related fixtures that already exist

`tests/fixtures/issue-repros/b102-array-sort-correct-callbacks/` and
`.../b102-array-sort-mismatched-callbacks/` were built for exactly this family.
**Neither covers a const handle**, which is why this survived. Add the const
positions to both — the correct one gains rows that must bind, the mismatched one
keeps the rows that must not.